    {
      "id": "u1",
      "title": "Sequences & Series",
      "unitTag": "Unit 1",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Arithmetic"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "a\u2099 = a\u2081 + (n \u2212 1)d"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "S\u2099 = n/2 \u00b7 (a\u2081 + a\u2099) = n/2 \u00b7 (2a\u2081 + (n\u22121)d)"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "d = common difference = a\u2082 \u2212 a\u2081 (constant gap)",
            "Finding n: sub known values into a\u2099 formula, solve for n",
            "Partial sum (terms i to j): S\u2c7c \u2212 S\u1d62\u208b\u2081"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Geometric"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "a\u2099 = a\u2081 \u00b7 r^(n\u22121)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "S\u2099 = a\u2081(1 \u2212 r\u207f) / (1 \u2212 r) [r \u2260 1]"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "S\u221e = a\u2081 / (1 \u2212 r) [only when |r| < 1]"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "r = common ratio = a\u2082/a\u2081",
            "Finding n: a\u2099 = a\u2081r\u207f\u207b\u00b9 \u2192 take log both sides \u2192 n = 1 + log(a\u2099/a\u2081)/log(r)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warn",
          "title": "Convergent vs Divergent \u2014 most tested concept",
          "items": [
            "Converges (S\u221e exists) iff |r| < 1",
            "Diverges (no S\u221e) iff |r| \u2265 1",
            "r = \u22120.99 \u2192 |r| = 0.99 < 1 \u2192 CONVERGES. Negative r \u2260 divergent.",
            "r = 1.01 \u2192 |r| = 1.01 \u2265 1 \u2192 DIVERGES."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sigma Notation"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "\u03a3\u1d62\u208c\u2081\u207f (a\u2081 + (i\u22121)d) \u2190 arithmetic sum to n terms"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "\u03a3\u1d62\u208c\u2081\u207f a\u2081\u00b7r^(i\u22121) \u2190 geometric sum to n terms"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "Bottom of sigma = starting index; top = ending index; expression = general term."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Find S\u221e",
          "steps": [
            "Q: \u03a3\u1d62\u208c\u2081^\u221e 8\u00b7(\u22120.5)^(i\u22121). Find S\u221e.",
            "1. a\u2081 = 8, r = \u22120.5",
            "2. |r| = 0.5 < 1 \u2192 converges",
            "3. S\u221e = 8 / (1\u2212(\u22120.5)) = 8/1.5 = 16/3 \u2248 5.33"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Find n",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Arithmetic seq, a\u2081=3, d=4. Which term equals 87?",
            "1. a\u2099 = 3 + (n\u22121)\u00b74 = 87",
            "2. (n\u22121)\u00b74 = 84 \u2192 n\u22121 = 21 \u2192 n = 22"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Two sums to find d",
          "steps": [
            "Q: 4th + 8th terms = 24, 6th + 10th terms = 44. Find the first three terms.",
            "1. (a\u2081+3d) + (a\u2081+7d) = 24 \u2192 a\u2081 + 5d = 12",
            "2. (a\u2081+5d) + (a\u2081+9d) = 44 \u2192 a\u2081 + 7d = 22",
            "3. Subtract (2) \u2212 (1): 2d = 10 \u2192 d = 5, a\u2081 = \u221213",
            "4. Terms: \u221213, \u22128, \u22123"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Radical difference and quadratic n",
          "steps": [
            "Q1: \u221a2 + \u221a8 + \u221a18 + \u2026 Sum of 13 terms.",
            "1. Simplify first: \u221a8 = 2\u221a2, \u221a18 = 3\u221a2 \u2192 d = \u221a2, a\u2081 = \u221a2",
            "2. S\u2081\u2083 = 13/2 (\u221a2 + 13\u221a2) = 91\u221a2",
            "Q2: How many terms of 2, 8, 14, \u2026 give a sum of 660?",
            "3. 660 = n/2 (4 + 6(n\u22121)) = 3n\u00b2 \u2212 n \u2192 3n\u00b2 \u2212 n \u2212 660 = 0",
            "4. n = (1 \u00b1 \u221a7921)/6 = (1 \u00b1 89)/6 \u2192 n = 15 or \u221288/6",
            "5. Reject the negative (n counts terms) \u2192 n = 15"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Geometric: same r either way",
          "steps": [
            "Q: x, x+5, x+15 is geometric. Find x.",
            "1. (x+5)/x = (x+15)/(x+5)",
            "2. Cross-multiply: (x+5)\u00b2 = x(x+15)",
            "3. x\u00b2 + 10x + 25 = x\u00b2 + 15x \u2192 25 = 5x \u2192 x = 5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Equal nth terms",
          "steps": [
            "Q: 162, 54, 18, \u2026 (r = 1/3) and 2/81, 2/27, 2/9, \u2026 (r = 3). Find n where they match.",
            "1. 162(1/3)\u207f\u207b\u00b9 = (2/81)(3)\u207f\u207b\u00b9",
            "2. (1/3)\u207f\u207b\u00b9 = 3^\u2212(n\u22121), so multiply both sides by 3\u207f\u207b\u00b9: 162 = (2/81)\u00b73^(2n\u22122)",
            "3. 162 \u00b7 81/2 = 6561 = 3\u2078 = 3^(2n\u22122) \u2192 n = 5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warn",
          "title": "Term or sum? Read what is asked",
          "items": [
            "Doubles every minute, files after 18 minutes: 1, 2, 4, 8\u2026 That is a single term, a\u2081\u2088 = 1\u00b72\u00b9\u2077 = 131 072, not S\u2081\u2088.",
            "Constant difference = arithmetic (d). Constant ratio = geometric (r). An infinite series written out must end in \u2026"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "title": "Tip",
          "items": [
            "If asked for \"sum of terms from term 5 to term 10\": compute S\u2081\u2080 \u2212 S\u2084. The \u22121 offset on the start matters."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u2",
      "title": "Transformations",
      "unitTag": "Unit 2",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "General Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "y = a \u00b7 f( b(x \u2212 h) ) + k"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Mapping rule: (x, y) \u2192 (x/b + h, a\u00b7y + k)"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Parameter",
            "Effect",
            "Trap"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "a",
              "Vertical stretch by |a|; a<0 reflects over x-axis",
              "|a|<1 = compression, not stretch"
            ],
            [
              "b",
              "Horizontal stretch by 1/|b|; b<0 reflects over y-axis",
              "b=2 means compress by \u00bd, NOT stretch by 2"
            ],
            [
              "h",
              "Shifts RIGHT by h (for positive h)",
              "y=f(x\u22123) shifts RIGHT 3, NOT left"
            ],
            [
              "k",
              "Shifts UP by k",
              "Nothing tricky here"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warn",
          "title": "h direction \u2014 most common error",
          "items": [
            "y = f(x \u2212 h) \u2192 shifts RIGHT. The h inside the bracket is subtracted. Positive h = right.",
            "y = f(x \u2212 3): h = 3, shifts 3 right. y = f(x + 3): h = \u22123, shifts 3 left."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Reflections"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Over x-axis: multiply outside \u2192 y = \u2212f(x)",
            "Over y-axis: negate x inside \u2192 y = f(\u2212x)",
            "Over y = x: swap x and y (this is the inverse)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Inverse Functions"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "To find f\u207b\u00b9(x): swap x and y, then solve for y"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Domain of f = Range of f\u207b\u00b9",
            "Range of f = Domain of f\u207b\u00b9",
            "Graph of f\u207b\u00b9 is reflection of f over the line y = x"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Identify Transformations",
          "steps": [
            "Q: y = \u22122f(3(x+1)) \u2212 4. State all transformations.",
            "1. a = \u22122 \u2192 vertical stretch \u00d72, reflect over x-axis",
            "2. b = 3 \u2192 horizontal compression to 1/3",
            "3. h = \u22121 \u2192 shift LEFT 1",
            "4. k = \u22124 \u2192 shift DOWN 4",
            "Mapping: (x,y) \u2192 (x/3 \u2212 1, \u22122y \u2212 4)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "title": "Tip \u2014 Applying the mapping rule",
          "items": [
            "Take key points from the original function. Apply (x/b+h, ay+k) to each. Mapping order: horizontal changes first (divide x by b, shift by h), then vertical (multiply y by a, shift by k)."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u3",
      "title": "Polynomials",
      "unitTag": "Unit 3",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Factoring Decision Tree"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Always factor GCF first",
            "2 terms: diff of squares or sum/diff of cubes",
            "3 terms, leading coeff = 1: simple trinomial (find p,q)",
            "3 terms, leading coeff \u2260 1: AC method",
            "4 terms: factor by grouping",
            "Degree 4 with x\u00b2 pattern: substitution (let u = x\u00b2)",
            "Degree 3+: rational root theorem + synthetic division"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Special Products"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "a\u00b2 \u2212 b\u00b2 = (a \u2212 b)(a + b)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "a\u00b3 + b\u00b3 = (a + b)(a\u00b2 \u2212 ab + b\u00b2)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "a\u00b3 \u2212 b\u00b3 = (a \u2212 b)(a\u00b2 + ab + b\u00b2)"
        },
        {
          "type": "warn",
          "title": "Cubes \u2014 middle sign trap",
          "items": [
            "Sum of cubes (a\u00b3+b\u00b3): middle term in trinomial is MINUS (a\u00b2\u2212ab+b\u00b2)",
            "Diff of cubes (a\u00b3\u2212b\u00b3): middle term is PLUS (a\u00b2+ab+b\u00b2)",
            "Memory aid: SOAP \u2014 Same, Opposite, Always Positive"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "AC Method (Messy Trinomials)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "ax\u00b2 + bx + c: find p,q where p\u00b7q = a\u00b7c AND p+q = b"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Split middle: ax\u00b2 + px + qx + c, then group"
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 AC Method",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Factor 6x\u00b2 \u2212 13x + 6",
            "1. ac = 6\u00b76 = 36. Find p,q: p\u00b7q=36, p+q=\u221213 \u2192 p=\u22129, q=\u22124",
            "2. 6x\u00b2 \u2212 9x \u2212 4x + 6",
            "3. 3x(2x \u2212 3) \u2212 2(2x \u2212 3)",
            "4. (3x \u2212 2)(2x \u2212 3)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Remainder & Factor Theorems"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "P(a) = remainder when P(x) \u00f7 (x \u2212 a) [Remainder Theorem]"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "P(a) = 0 \u2194 (x \u2212 a) is a factor [Factor Theorem]"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Rational roots: \u00b1(factors of constant) / (factors of leading coeff)"
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Factor Cubic",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Factor P(x) = x\u00b3 \u2212 5x\u00b2 \u2212 12x + 36",
            "1. Try rational roots: \u00b11,\u00b12,\u00b13,\u00b14,\u00b16,\u00b19,\u00b112,\u00b118,\u00b136",
            "2. P(2) = 8\u221220\u221224+36 = 0 \u2713 \u2192 (x\u22122) is a factor",
            "3. Synthetic division \u2192 x\u00b3\u22125x\u00b2\u221212x+36 = (x\u22122)(x\u00b2\u22123x\u221218)",
            "4. x\u00b2\u22123x\u221218 = (x\u22126)(x+3)",
            "5. P(x) = (x\u22122)(x\u22126)(x+3)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Polynomial Graph Characteristics"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Degree n \u2192 max n x-intercepts, max n\u22121 turning points",
            "Leading coeff +, even degree \u2192 both ends go UP (U shape)",
            "Leading coeff \u2212, even degree \u2192 both ends go DOWN",
            "Leading coeff +, odd degree \u2192 left DOWN, right UP",
            "Leading coeff \u2212, odd degree \u2192 left UP, right DOWN",
            "Odd multiplicity at root \u2192 graph CROSSES x-axis",
            "Even multiplicity at root \u2192 graph BOUNCES off x-axis"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u4",
      "title": "Exponents & Logs",
      "unitTag": "Unit 4",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Exponent Laws"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "x\u1d43 \u00b7 x\u1d47 = x\u1d43\u207a\u1d47 (product \u2014 add exponents)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "x\u1d43 / x\u1d47 = x\u1d43\u207b\u1d47 (quotient \u2014 subtract)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "(x\u1d43)\u1d47 = x\u1d43\u1d47 (power of power \u2014 multiply)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "x\u2070 = 1 (zero exponent)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "x\u207b\u207f = 1/x\u207f (negative \u2014 flip)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "x^(m/n) = \u207f\u221a(x\u1d50) (rational \u2014 root of power)"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Logarithms"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "log_b(A) = E \u2194 b\u1d31 = A (definition: log = exponent)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "log_b(AB) = log_b(A) + log_b(B) (product rule)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "log_b(A/B) = log_b(A) \u2212 log_b(B) (quotient rule)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "log_b(A\u207f) = n \u00b7 log_b(A) (power rule)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "log_b(x) = log(x) / log(b) (change of base)"
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "title": "Log = \"what exponent do I need?\"",
          "items": [
            "log\u2082(8) = 3 because 2\u00b3 = 8. The log is the exponent. B = base, E = exponent, A = answer (result)."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Solving Exponential Equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Same base: 2^(x+1) = 2^5 \u2192 x+1 = 5 \u2192 x = 4",
            "Different base: take log both sides, use power rule"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "3^x = 10 \u2192 x\u00b7log3 = log10 \u2192 x = log10/log3 \u2248 2.096"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Growth & Decay"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "A = A\u2080 \u00b7 b^(t/p) [b = growth factor, p = period for one cycle]"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "A = A\u2080 \u00b7 (1/2)^(t/h) [half-life: h = half-life period]"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Log Scale Applications"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Richter: difference in readings = exponent of 10\u00d7 power. \u0394=3.8 \u2192 10\u00b3\u00b7\u2078\u22486310\u00d7 more powerful",
            "pH: pH = \u2212log[H\u207a]; more acidic = lower pH; ratio of acidity = 10^(pH\u2082\u2212pH\u2081)",
            "Decibels: dB = 10\u00b7log(I/I\u2080); increase of 10 dB = 10\u00d7 louder"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Change of Base",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Solve 5^x = 500",
            "1. log both sides: x\u00b7log5 = log500",
            "2. x = log500/log5",
            "3. x = 2.699/0.699 \u2248 3.86"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Log Rules Combined",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Write log x + 5 log y \u2212 3 log x as single log",
            "1. Combine x terms: (1\u22123)logx = \u22122logx",
            "2. Power rule: log(x\u207b\u00b2) + log(y\u2075)",
            "3. Product rule: log(y\u2075/x\u00b2)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u5",
      "title": "Rational Expressions",
      "unitTag": "Unit 5",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Fundamentals"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "A rational expression is polynomial / polynomial . The denominator cannot equal zero."
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Non-permissible values: set denominator = 0 and solve \u2192 those are EXCLUDED"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Operations"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Operation",
            "Method"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Simplify",
              "Factor top and bottom fully, cancel common factors, state restrictions"
            ],
            [
              "Multiply",
              "Factor everything, cancel across numerators/denominators, multiply remaining"
            ],
            [
              "Divide",
              "Multiply by reciprocal of second fraction, then same as multiply"
            ],
            [
              "Add/Subtract",
              "Find LCD (LCM of denominators), convert each fraction, combine numerators, simplify"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warn",
          "title": "Restrictions after cancelling",
          "items": [
            "State ALL non-permissible values from the ORIGINAL denominators \u2014 even if a factor was cancelled. Restrictions come from before simplification."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Graphing y = a/(x\u2212h) + k"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Vertical asymptote: x = h (denominator = 0)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Horizontal asymptote: y = k (what y approaches as x \u2192 \u00b1\u221e)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "a: steepness; a > 0 \u2192 Q1/Q3 relative to asymptotes; a < 0 \u2192 Q2/Q4"
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "title": "Cheat code \u2014 finding 'a' from a graph",
          "items": [
            "Pick any obvious grid point on the graph. Sub its (x,y) into y = a/(x\u2212h)+k and solve for a. Always works."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Solving Rational Equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Identify all non-permissible values first",
            "Multiply every term by the LCD to clear fractions",
            "Solve the resulting polynomial equation",
            "Check solutions against non-permissible values \u2014 reject any that match"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Find Equation from Graph",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Graph has VA at x=3, HA at y=2, passes through (4,7)",
            "1. h=3, k=2. Form: y = a/(x\u22123) + 2",
            "2. Sub (4,7): 7 = a/(4\u22123) + 2 = a + 2",
            "3. a = 5 \u2192 y = 5/(x\u22123) + 2"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u6",
      "title": "Sinusoidal Functions",
      "unitTag": "Unit 6",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "General Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "y = a \u00b7 sin( b(x \u2212 h) ) + k"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "y = a \u00b7 cos( b(x \u2212 h) ) + k"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Parameter",
            "What it does",
            "How to find from graph"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "a",
              "Amplitude = |a|; a<0 flips vertically",
              "a = (max \u2212 min) / 2"
            ],
            [
              "b",
              "Period = 2\u03c0/|b| (in radians) or 360\u00b0/|b| (degrees)",
              "b = 2\u03c0 / period"
            ],
            [
              "h",
              "Phase shift: moves graph right by h",
              "Read from graph where cycle starts"
            ],
            [
              "k",
              "Vertical displacement (midline = y=k)",
              "k = (max + min) / 2"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Graphing \u2014 5 Key Points Method"
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "For one full period of y = a\u00b7sin(b(x\u2212h))+k:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Start at phase shift h: y = k (zero)",
            "Quarter period: y = k+a (max)",
            "Half period: y = k (zero)",
            "Three-quarter period: y = k\u2212a (min)",
            "Full period: y = k (zero, cycle repeats)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "p",
          "text": "For cosine: starts at max (k+a), not zero."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Equation from Graph",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Graph has max=7, min=1, period=4\u03c0, starts at x=\u03c0",
            "1. a = (7\u22121)/2 = 3",
            "2. k = (7+1)/2 = 4",
            "3. b = 2\u03c0/4\u03c0 = 1/2",
            "4. h = \u03c0 (phase shift right)",
            "5. y = 3\u00b7sin(\u00bd(x\u2212\u03c0)) + 4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "title": "Radians vs Degrees",
          "items": [
            "Standard PC12 uses radians. Period formula: T = 2\u03c0/|b|. If period is given as \"360\u00b0\" then in radians that is 2\u03c0. Always match units."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warn",
          "title": "Phase shift direction",
          "items": [
            "y = sin(x \u2212 \u03c0/3): phase shift = +\u03c0/3 (shifts RIGHT). Same h-direction trap as U2 transformations."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "u7",
      "title": "Trig Identities",
      "unitTag": "Unit 7",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Foundational Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Reciprocal: csc \u03b8 = 1/sin \u03b8 sec \u03b8 = 1/cos \u03b8 cot \u03b8 = 1/tan \u03b8"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Quotient: tan \u03b8 = sin \u03b8/cos \u03b8 cot \u03b8 = cos \u03b8/sin \u03b8"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Pythagorean: sin\u00b2\u03b8 + cos\u00b2\u03b8 = 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "Derived: tan\u00b2\u03b8 + 1 = sec\u00b2\u03b8 1 + cot\u00b2\u03b8 = csc\u00b2\u03b8"
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "title": "Deriving Pythagorean identities",
          "items": [
            "Start from sin\u00b2\u03b8+cos\u00b2\u03b8=1. Divide both sides by cos\u00b2\u03b8 \u2192 tan\u00b2\u03b8+1=sec\u00b2\u03b8. Divide by sin\u00b2\u03b8 \u2192 1+cot\u00b2\u03b8=csc\u00b2\u03b8. Don't memorize three \u2014 derive from one."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Proving Identities \u2014 Strategy"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Work ONE side only \u2014 never move terms across the = sign",
            "Pick the more complex side to transform",
            "Common techniques (in order to try):"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Rewrite everything in terms of sin and cos",
            "Factor out common terms",
            "Get a common denominator to combine fractions",
            "Multiply top and bottom by conjugate (1+sin\u03b8, 1\u2212cos\u03b8, etc.)",
            "Use a Pythagorean identity to substitute (sin\u00b2=1\u2212cos\u00b2 etc.)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warn",
          "title": "Never cross-multiply to \"prove\"",
          "items": [
            "Moving terms across the = sign assumes what you're trying to prove. Work one side only."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sum & Difference Formulas"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "sin(A \u00b1 B) = sinA\u00b7cosB \u00b1 cosA\u00b7sinB"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "cos(A \u00b1 B) = cosA\u00b7cosB \u2213 sinA\u00b7sinB (note: \u2213 flips the sign)"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "tan(A \u00b1 B) = (tanA \u00b1 tanB) / (1 \u2213 tanA\u00b7tanB)"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Double Angle Formulas"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "sin(2A) = 2\u00b7sinA\u00b7cosA"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "cos(2A) = cos\u00b2A \u2212 sin\u00b2A = 1 \u2212 2sin\u00b2A = 2cos\u00b2A \u2212 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "text": "tan(2A) = 2\u00b7tanA / (1 \u2212 tan\u00b2A)"
        },
        {
          "type": "tip",
          "title": "Three forms of cos(2A)",
          "items": [
            "All three are equivalent via sin\u00b2+cos\u00b2=1. Pick whichever form makes the target expression cleanest. If the target has only cos, use 2cos\u00b2A\u22121."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Solving Trig Equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            "Isolate the trig function (treat sin\u03b8 or cos\u03b8 like a variable)",
            "Find the reference angle using inverse trig",
            "Identify which quadrants give the correct sign",
            "List all solutions in the given domain",
            "General solution: add 2\u03c0n (for sin/cos period 2\u03c0) or \u03c0n (for tan period \u03c0)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked Example \u2014 Solving with Identity",
          "steps": [
            "Q: Solve 2sin\u00b2\u03b8 \u2212 sin\u03b8 \u2212 1 = 0, \u03b8 \u2208 [0, 2\u03c0]",
            "1. Factor: (2sin\u03b8 + 1)(sin\u03b8 \u2212 1) = 0",
            "2. sin\u03b8 = \u2212\u00bd \u2192 \u03b8 = 7\u03c0/6, 11\u03c0/6",
            "3. sin\u03b8 = 1 \u2192 \u03b8 = \u03c0/2",
            "Solutions: \u03b8 = \u03c0/2, 7\u03c0/6, 11\u03c0/6"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
